    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d252">

                <pb facs="0401" n="289" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg289"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1361</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3667</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-07-03">July 3, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received July
                        27.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Legation’s
                    despatch No. 3375 of February 19, 1935,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d252-f1"/> in
                    regard to the Shanghai Defense Scheme and to enclose for the Department’s
                    information copies of the Shanghai <pb facs="0402" n="290" xml:id="pg290"/>
                    Consul General’s despatches to the Legation No. 8445 of April 25, No. 8710 of
                    June 26,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d252-f2"/> and No. 8719 of June 29, 1935,<anchor
                        n="14" xml:id="d252-f3"/> together with a copy of the Legation’s instruction
                    to the Consul General of July 3, 1935.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d252-f4"/></p>
                <p>The Department will note that Rear Admiral Araki, Commanding the Japanese Naval
                    Landing Party at Shanghai, suggested to the other Garrison Commanders a revision
                    of Section (<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>) of paragraph 2 of Part 2 of the
                    Defense Scheme regarding the passage through the Settlement of Chinese troops,
                    which would incorporate the Sino-Japanese Armistice of May 5, 1932,<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d252-f5"/> therein, and would serve the purpose of
                    authorizing a condition similar to that which existed in 1932 in the Hongkew
                    district, but that the Commander of the Fourth Marines in Shanghai and the
                    Secretary General of the Municipal Council have replied to the Japanese
                    Commander to the effect that they could not agree to the proposed amendment.</p>
                <p>The Legation has replied to the Consul General to the effect that it concurs in
                    the views expressed by Colonel Beaumont and by the Secretary General of the
                    Municipal Council, and that it desires that he maintain a similar attitude
                    toward any attempt of the Japanese to obtain his approval of any claim of prime
                    interest which they may consider that they possess in Hongkew or any other
                    district of Shanghai.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d252-a1.xml" xpointer="d252-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
